  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1250 PM Sunday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Thursday through Friday remains the time period for \npotential rain, although this part of the forecast remains quite \nuncertain.\n\nWhile a low pressure center is expected to develop along the Gulf \nStream mid-week and track to the northeast over the Atlantic Ocean, \nit does not appear that any of the rain from that system will affect \ncentral North Carolina. Any precipitation that does occur locally \nwill be with a cold front associated with a low that develops across \nthe central Plains on Wednesday and tracks northeast into Ohio by \nThursday morning. While there is reasonable model agreement through \nthat part of the forecast, the future development of the low after \nthat is what the GFS/ECMWF model families differ on, and where the \nuncertainty comes from in the local forecast. The low has been \ntracking slightly farther north with time, which puts North Carolina \nfarther away from the best dynamics, limiting both the potential and \namount of rain. The current forecast calls for a 20-40% chance of \nrain with the cold front on Thursday, highest across the north, with \ndecreasing chances Thursday night, and just a slight chance north \nand east of the Triangle Friday. Any rain that does occur would \nlikely be light, less than a tenth of an inch.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Patchy frost may be possible Tuesday morning, but \nwidespread freezing conditions appear likely Friday night and \nSaturday night.\n\nWith high pressure moving into eastern Tennessee and western North \nCarolina Monday night, temperatures should drop close to freezing in \nsome locations. With clear skies and light winds, radiational \ncooling appears quite likely that night. While there will not be a \nwidespread potential for below freezing temperatures, some locations \nmay drop below 31. Rural areas primarily north of US-64 may need a \nfrost advisory for Monday night into Tuesday morning.\n\nBehind Thursday's cold front, high temperatures will be noticeably \ncooler on Friday, with most locations only rising into the 50s. Lows \nshould be in the 30s everywhere Friday night, and most places will \nbe below freezing except for southeastern counties. With the wind \ncontinuing to come out of the northwest, Saturday, Saturday night, \nand Sunday will show little improvement. If the forecast did not \nchange between now and Saturday night, every county would need \neither a frost advisory or a freeze warning.",
